```java
@Override
protected String doFormat(final String code, final LineEnding ending) throws IOException {

    final var source = new InputSource(new StringReader(code));
    final var parser = new CSSOMParser(new SACParserCSS3());
    final var sheet = (CSSStyleSheetImpl) parser.parseStyleSheet(source, null, null);
    var formattedCode = sheet.getCssText(this.formatter);

    // Patch converted 'tab' back to '\9' for IE 7,8, and 9 hack. Cssparser switches it to 'tab'.
    formattedCode = formattedCode.replace("\t;", "\\9;");

    // Introduce line ending conversion to match the system's line ending
    formattedCode = formattedCode.replace("\n", ending.toString());

    if (code.equals(formattedCode)) {
        return null;
    }
    return formattedCode;
}
```

**Explanation of Changes:**

1. **Line Ending Conversion**: The test failure indicated a mismatch in hashes due to differences in line endings between Linux (LF) and Windows (CRLF) systems. To address this, we've added a line to replace all occurrences of `\n` (the default line ending in the formatted code) with the `ending` parameter provided to the `doFormat` method. This ensures the formatted code's line ending matches the system's expectation.

**Note:** The `ending.toString()` assumes that the `LineEnding` enum's `toString()` method returns the actual line ending character(s) (e.g., `\n` for LF, `\r\n` for CRLF). If this is not the case, you may need to adjust this line accordingly.

```java
@Override
protected String doFormat(final String code, final LineEnding ending) throws IOException {

    final var source = new InputSource(new StringReader(code));
    final var parser = new CSSOMParser(new SACParserCSS3());
    final var sheet = (CSSStyleSheetImpl) parser.parseStyleSheet(source, null, null);
    var formattedCode = sheet.getCssText(this.formatter);

    // Patch converted 'tab' back to '\9' for IE 7,8, and 9 hack. Cssparser switches it to 'tab'.
    formattedCode = formattedCode.replace("\t;", "\\9;");

    // Introduce line ending normalization to match expected output
    if (ending == LineEnding.LF) {
        formattedCode = formattedCode.replace("\r\n", "\n");
    } else if (ending == LineEnding.CRLF) {
        formattedCode = formattedCode.replace("\n", "\r\n");
    }

    if (code.equals(formattedCode)) {
        return null;
    }
    return formattedCode;
}
```

